\description{
CDC suppresses small county counts. \code{impute_suppressed()} gives each
suppressed county a count so county data can be added up or shared across
census tracts with \code{\link[=extrapolate_from_county]{extrapolate_from_county()}}. Run it on county data from
\code{\link[=get_atlas]{get_atlas()}} before \code{\link[=no_county_cases]{no_county_cases()}} or \code{\link[=extrapolate_from_county]{extrapolate_from_county()}}.

Rows are handled by what CDC published:
\itemize{
\item A published count is kept, whatever its data status. (Connecticut's 2017
planning regions are marked "Data not available" but have counts.)
\item A row marked "Data not available" with no count has no data to estimate
from; \code{not_available} says whether it is kept or dropped. Its cases are
still in the state total, so \code{\link[=no_county_cases]{no_county_cases()}} counts them in the
remainder, as long as the state still has a row in that indicator, year
and stratum.
\item A row marked "Data suppressed" with no count is filled in by \code{method}.
\item A row with no count and any other status (or none), such as "No reported
cases" or a code this version doesn't know, is not filled in: it stays
\code{NA} and is marked \code{"not estimated"}, with a warning.
}
}
\details{
Filled-in counts are estimates, not whole numbers, and are not rounded:
\code{"state_rate"} can give 1.6 cases, for example. Each stratum is filled in,
capped and floored on its own, from the state rate in that stratum, so a
county's filled-in strata need not add up to its filled-in unstratified
count. With the default cap of 4, two suppressed sex strata can add up to
8 while the same county's unstratified estimate is at most 4.

\code{"state_rate"} downloads the state data matching \code{x} (same diseases, years
and stratification) from CDC AtlasPlus, and with \code{population = "acs"} the
tract populations from the Census. \code{fit_to = "county_total"} by sex or
age also downloads CDC's unstratified county data for the states and
years in \code{x} (one request per state and year, saved and reused), and CDC's
county data matching \code{x} for the state check, which is usually already
saved from \code{\link[=get_atlas]{get_atlas()}}.
}
